# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

## Overview
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the invoice-management project using the latest official documentation.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package (version resolved via npm)
- Used npm as the project's package manager
- Package added to dependencies in package.json

### 2. Analytics Integration
**Modified: `src/App.tsx`**
- Added import for Analytics component: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/react';`
- Integrated the Analytics component into the main App component
- Placed Analytics component alongside RouterProvider using a React Fragment
- Follows Vite/React framework-specific instructions from Vercel documentation

## Implementation Details

The Analytics component was added to the root App component, which is the appropriate location for a Vite + React application. This ensures analytics tracking is initialized for all pages/routes handled by the React Router.

The implementation follows the official Vercel Analytics quickstart guide (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart) for React/Vite projects.

## Code Structure
```jsx
<>
  <RouterProvider router={router} />
  <Analytics />
</>
```

This structure ensures:
- Analytics is loaded on application mount
- All route navigations are tracked
- Minimal impact on existing code structure

## Verification Performed
- ✅ TypeScript type checking passed (`npm run typecheck`)
- ✅ Production build completed successfully (`npm run build`)
- ✅ No new lint errors introduced by the changes
- ✅ Existing lint errors are pre-existing and unrelated to this implementation

## Next Steps
To enable analytics data collection:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics section)
2. Deploy the application to Vercel
3. Verify analytics are working by checking the Network tab for requests to `/<unique-path>/view`

## Files Modified
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `src/App.tsx` - Added Analytics component

## Notes
- The project's .gitignore intentionally excludes package-lock.json, so it was not staged
- Build artifacts (dist/) were not staged per standard practices
- All changes preserve existing code structure and functionality
